ds:concept.Question-mark-vs-information-icon a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Question mark vs information icon";
    ds:summary "*This concept is a stub.*\n\nThis article will help you decide between using the information icon or the question mark icon in your application when you want to signal to users that there is more information available on a specific topic. ";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "The information icon signals 'Here is additional context you might find useful,' making it a bit more proactive in offering supplementary details. The question mark icon signals 'Need help understanding this?', making it a bit more reactive and assuming that the user might be confused or unfamiliar with the concept.\n\nThere is a soft convention is that “the question mark icon indicates extensive help is available, provides an interface for someone having a problem to click, and implies that a more sophisticated means of resolving the problem is being offered. The information icon indicates only that some additional explanatory information is available, but not an extensive help system.”1  \n  \nWith the `i` the system offers the additional information to the user, that may be useful even when he does not have a question.\n\nSo  `i`  is the right icon to use if you have information that:\n\n- the user would not ask for, because he's not missing it\n- is useful for a user who is interested in the details.\n\nInformation exposed only through a tooltip must never be essential to completing the task.\n\n\n\nWith the `**?**`, the system offers the information to the user, that he needs when he has a question.\n\nSo  `?`  is the right icon to use if you have information that:\n\n- would unblock the user\n- would offer extensive supplimentary help\n\nIcons depend heavily on the visual conventions a user has been exposed to, so how they interpret an icon will often come down to their cultural background and prior experience. Ultimately, the accompanying text or tooltip is what communicates our intention to the user. However some additional internal research has proven the above assumptions.\n\n  \nThe interaction and triggered component also varies depending on the provided information. This is how to decide what to do:\n\nIs the information necessary to complete the task?  \n│  \n├── YES  \n│   └── Don't hide it in a tooltip.  \n│       Use visible helper text / inline guidance.  \n│  \n└── NO  \n    │  \n    Is it brief and contextual?  \n    │  \n    ├── YES  \n    │   └── i + tooltip  \n    │  \n    └── NO  \n        │  \n        Is it contextual and can be understood  \n        without leaving the current task?  \n        │  \n        ├── YES  \n        │   └── i + toggletip/popover  \n        │  \n        └── NO  \n            └── ? icon button + link to help/documentation\n\n### Source\n\n1. [https://ux.stackexchange.com/questions/64920/info-icon-vs-question-mark](https://ux.stackexchange.com/questions/64920/info-icon-vs-question-mark)\n\n\n\n### References\n\n[https://www.nngroup.com/articles/info-tips-bad/](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/info-tips-bad/)\n\n[https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-std-icons?redirectedfrom=MSDN](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-std-icons?redirectedfrom=MSDN)\n\n[https://ux.stackexchange.com/questions/80954/is-the-question-mark-internationally-recognised-as-an-icon-for-help](https://ux.stackexchange.com/questions/80954/is-the-question-mark-internationally-recognised-as-an-icon-for-help)\n\n[https://ux.stackexchange.com/questions/21102/best-uses-of-help-and-i-information-icons-in-ui](https://ux.stackexchange.com/questions/21102/best-uses-of-help-and-i-information-icons-in-ui)\n\n[https://uxmovement.com/forms/question-mark-vs-info-icon-when-to-use-which/](https://uxmovement.com/forms/question-mark-vs-info-icon-when-to-use-which/)\n\n";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.How-to-guide.
